WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:09.620
The T cell receptor reads a short peptide held in the groove of an MHC protein.

2
00:00:10.740 --> 00:00:16.100
Blocking a brake helps only if the patient already has T cells that recognise the

3
00:00:16.100 --> 00:00:20.340
tumour. The other approach gives T cells a new receptor.

4
00:00:21.100 --> 00:00:22.240
Can you build one from parts?

5
00:00:23.150 --> 00:00:31.350
In 1989, Gideon Gross, Tova Waks and Zelig Eshhar joined the variable regions of an

6
00:00:31.350 --> 00:00:35.390
antibody to the constant regions of T cell receptor chains.

7
00:00:36.410 --> 00:00:40.170
T cells carrying the hybrid responded to the antibody's target.

8
00:00:41.390 --> 00:00:46.630
Yoshihisa Kuwana and colleagues had published the same kind of construct in 1987.

9
00:00:47.700 --> 00:00:53.400
The receptor's own tips read a peptide held on an MHC protein.

10
00:00:54.380 --> 00:00:56.000
An antibody is different.

11
00:00:56.660 --> 00:00:59.560
It grips a whole folded protein directly.

12
00:01:00.420 --> 00:01:04.840
So such a receptor needs no peptide and no MHC protein.

13
00:01:05.683 --> 00:01:10.523
Later designs, from Eshhar's laboratory and others, were single chains.

14
00:01:11.283 --> 00:01:15.803
They put an antibody fragment outside the cell and the zeta tail inside.

15
00:01:16.683 --> 00:01:20.103
This is a chimeric antigen receptor, or CAR.

16
00:01:21.203 --> 00:01:25.163
Remember the plan: antibody outside, zeta tail inside.
